In message <clu6hm$9t0$1@news3.zwoll1.ov.home.nl>, "Boudewijn Waijers" wrote:
..
> > This patch would allow us to do a "Fastest Ascension" trophy, but
> > only in terms of gametime.  It's been suggested that a matching
> > trophy for realtime would also be good.
> 
> What'd be the added value of knowing how much free time someone has at
> hand, how quickly he can type, and how fast someone's internet
> connection is?
> 
> In my opinion, realtime says nothing about someone's playing ability.

Good point.

Certainly the speed of the Internet connection would affect it, and I hadn't
considered that, but at the very least realtime should distinguish among folks
who can blaze through the game and those (like me) who have to stop to consider
our moves and sometimes consult a document to figure out what to do.

Gametime, as someone pointed out, also reflects an element of luck (as opposed
to skill) in that an early acquisition of speed could significantly reduce the
turns needed to ascend.  I do get the impression from conversations here,
though, that a _really_ good player wouldn't be affected by this much.

It's difficult: pretty much _any_ measure we can choose for a Tournament will
be affected by things other than skill.  Our goal has been to choose a variety
of things on which to base the winning positions since no _one_ measure can
capture the complexity of the game.

It's certainly possible that I'm wrong, and there would be no interesting
measure resulting from capturing realtime expired per game.  At the least,
though, I think it'd be interesting to see whether it would be interesting, if
that makes any sense.  ;-)

-robin

